\ A recover inside a cleanup quotation consumes its own error, and only its own. The body's
\ error is still unwinding through the same context, so its type, message, and frames must
\ reach the renderer intact. Before the consumption bound, the recover's capture folded the
\ body's pended frames into the caught error and then cleared them, and the body error
\ degraded to a bare `error.user-thrown` line with no chain at all.
\
\ No flags, so the interpreted and eager suites bind one golden.

boom: ( -- ) [ f "body failed" body-error: make-error throw ] ;
oops: ( -- ) [ f "cleanup caught this" cleanup-error: make-error throw ] ;

[ boom ] [ [ oops ] [ drop ] recover ] cleanup
